From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Elena Maher <12779416+elenaOnline@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 06:58:34 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] gpui: Prepare glyph rasters ahead of painting them, off the
 main thread

Painting a glyph the sprite atlas does not have rasterizes it on the main
thread: the platform draws it into a bitmap (on macOS Core Text, and for a
font at a new size a font registry query first). Text that keeps changing
size, such as a canvas's while it zooms, reaches new sizes every frame, and
a text-heavy canvas spent most of such a frame rasterizing.

`TextSystem::prepare_glyphs` rasterizes glyphs on the calling thread (a
background one) and keeps copies of the rasters; painting a glyph with the
same parameters takes its prepared raster instead of rasterizing (once).
The copies live in 1 MB chunks mapped from the system (allocated on other
platforms), 16 MB at most: a new chunk past that takes the place of the
oldest, whose rasters are dropped, and `TextSystem::release_prepared_glyphs`
drops them all and unmaps the chunks, so the memory leaves the process at
once (an allocator may keep what one thread frees of another's, and return
it to the system lazily; the rasterizing thread frees its own bitmaps). A
glyph painting rasterized itself while glyphs were being prepared (not
prepared in time) is not prepared again, or kept, until the release. A
glyph prepared twice at once (two threads) keeps one raster. A glyph whose
raster would take more than 256 KB is not prepared: painting rasterizes it
as before. `TextSystem::prepared_glyph_stats` counts what became of the
prepared rasters (prepared, taken, dropped, rasterized too late, not kept
for want of memory, and their bytes), for callers that adapt how much they
ask for.

`TextSystem::rasterize_glyph` (what painting calls) is public, and
`Window::glyph_render_params` gives the parameters
`paint_glyph_transformed` would rasterize a glyph with now (the subpixel
variant, the dilation for the colour, the font smoothing), so a caller can
ask for exactly the rasters its next frames will paint;
`Window::has_glyph_raster` tells whether the sprite atlas has a glyph
(`PlatformAtlas::contains`, now outside test support; the Metal, wgpu
and DirectX atlases answer it), so a caller does not ask for those. Nothing changes for
callers that do not prepare glyphs: the same raster comes out either way.

`TextSystem::raster_bounds` no longer holds its cache's upgradable lock
while it measures a glyph, so glyphs measured on other threads do not hold
up the ones painted meanwhile.

